\citation{yang2021simam}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces MobilePIXORNeXt feedforward hierarchy with layer dimensions, channel widths, and receptive fields. A two-layer stem processes 14-channel input at stride 2 ($400 \times 352$). Three stages follow: C2 has 48 channels and depth 3; C3 has 96 channels and depth 4; C4 has 128 channels and depth 2. Each stage uses \texttt  {MobilePIXORNeXt-S} blocks with Block-SimAM. The Scale-Gated FPN (SG-FPN) uses lateral $1 \times 1$ projections, bilinear $2\times $ upsampling, depthwise $3 \times 3$ refinement, and learnable scale gates $\mathbf  {G}_r$. It produces a shared 32-channel feature map ($F_{32}$) at stride 4 ($200 \times 176$). Independent grouped towers separate Car predictions from Pedestrian and Cyclist predictions to reduce aspect-ratio conflicts within the parameter budget (753,569 total parameters).}}{9}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:backbone}{{3}{9}{MobilePIXORNeXt feedforward hierarchy with layer dimensions, channel widths, and receptive fields. A two-layer stem processes 14-channel input at stride 2 ($400 \times 352$). Three stages follow: C2 has 48 channels and depth 3; C3 has 96 channels and depth 4; C4 has 128 channels and depth 2. Each stage uses \texttt {MobilePIXORNeXt-S} blocks with Block-SimAM. The Scale-Gated FPN (SG-FPN) uses lateral $1 \times 1$ projections, bilinear $2\times $ upsampling, depthwise $3 \times 3$ refinement, and learnable scale gates $\mathbf {G}_r$. It produces a shared 32-channel feature map ($F_{32}$) at stride 4 ($200 \times 176$). Independent grouped towers separate Car predictions from Pedestrian and Cyclist predictions to reduce aspect-ratio conflicts within the parameter budget (753,569 total parameters)}{figure.3}{}}
